UNIT pullAt
KIND STATE
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG pullAt(array, ...indexes) -> Array   # module.exports default; variadic; each `indexes` arg may be a number, a numeric-string, or an Array of such (flattened ONE level)

DEPENDS-ON none   # self-contained: all helpers (baseRest, baseFlatten, baseAt, basePullAt, get, isIndex, arrayMap, compareAscending) are bundled in the single source file and reproduced as in-contract logic below

# =====================================================================
# OBSERVATION CONTRACT  (SIR v0.2 §11 — KIND STATE, RETURN != POST)
# =====================================================================
# The factory auto-stamps observe='result+post'. The observable is the PAIR:
#     result = the RETURNED array of removed-element VALUES (a NEW array)
#     post   = the SAME `array` object after IN-PLACE mutation (elements removed)
# Both must match. The whole reason this unit exists in the differential is that
# RETURN != POST: a do-nothing impostor that returns the right `result` but does
# NOT mutate `array` (post unchanged) MUST FAIL on `post`. Conversely an impostor
# that mutates correctly but returns the wrong removed-values MUST FAIL on `result`.
#
# IDENTITY: the EXACT array object passed in is mutated (Array.prototype.splice is
# called on it / properties deleted from it); a fresh array is NOT substituted.
# `result` is a brand-new Array distinct from `array`.

# =====================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline — exact + complete)
# =====================================================================
# Let the call be  pullAt(array, arg1, arg2, ..., argK).
#
# NODE 1 — REST GATHER (baseRest, start = func.length-1 = 1):
#   Collect every argument after `array` into one array:
#       indexes = [arg1, arg2, ..., argK]
#   (If only `array` is passed, indexes = [].)
#
# NODE 2 — FLATTEN ONE LEVEL (baseFlatten(indexes, 1)):
#   Replace `indexes` with a one-level flatten. For each element E of indexes:
#     - if E is "flattenable" (E is an Array, OR an `arguments` object, OR a
#       Symbol.isConcatSpreadable object): SPREAD its elements into the result
#       (one level only — nested arrays are NOT recursively flattened; a nested
#       array element survives as an array element).
#     - otherwise: push E unchanged.
#   Result is a flat list `idxs` whose elements are the actual index values.
#   # In-scope generator inputs use only numbers / numeric-strings, optionally
#   # wrapped one level in a plain Array. So this node turns e.g. [0,[2,3],5]
#   # into [0,2,3,5]. (See OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for the array-valued-element path.)
#
# NODE 3 — CAPTURE LENGTH (BEFORE any mutation):
#       length = array ? array.length : 0
#
# NODE 4 — COMPUTE result  (baseAt(array, idxs)) — RETURN VALUE, computed PRE-mutation:
#   result = new Array of the SAME length as idxs, where for k = 0..idxs.length-1:
#       result[k] = get(array, idxs[k])
#   `get(array, idx)` semantics for in-scope idx values:
#       - idx a number n that is an integer in [0, length):  result[k] = array[n]
#       - idx a numeric STRING "n" naming an existing own index: result[k] = array[n]
#       - idx out-of-range (n >= length), NEGATIVE (n < 0), fractional (1.5),
#         or otherwise not an own property: result[k] = undefined
#   *** result PRESERVES THE ORIGINAL (post-flatten) ORDER OF idxs — it is NOT sorted. ***
#   *** undefined elements ARE produced for out-of-range / negative / fractional /
#       missing indexes (one undefined per such index). They are real `undefined`
#       array elements (JSON-serialize to null). ***
#   *** No array element is read after removal: every read here uses the ORIGINAL,
#       still-unmutated `array`. ***
#
# NODE 5 — BUILD SORTED REMOVAL LIST:
#   sorted = arrayMap(idxs, idx => isIndex(idx, length) ? +idx : idx)
#            .sort(compareAscending)
#   - isIndex(value, length):  length != 0  AND
#       (typeof value == 'number'  OR  /^(?:0|[1-9]\d*)$/.test(String(value)))  AND
#       (value > -1  AND  value % 1 == 0  AND  value < length)
#       # i.e. value is a non-negative integer strictly < length (numbers, or
#       # canonical unsigned-integer strings like "0","1","12"; NOT "01", NOT
#       # "-1", NOT "1.5"). For an in-range index this maps it to its NUMBER form (+idx).
#       # Out-of-range / negative / fractional / non-canonical-string indexes are
#       # left AS-IS (not coerced).
#   - compareAscending(a, b): ascending total order. For the in-scope value set
#     (numbers + numeric strings, all mapped to numbers when in range) it is plain
#     ascending by `<`/`>`; equal values compare 0; undefined / NaN / null sort to
#     the END (return 1 when `a` is the "greater"/non-reflexive side). For in-scope
#     numeric indexes this is just an ascending numeric sort.
#   # Net effect: in-range indexes become numbers and are sorted ascending; they are
#   # then consumed back-to-front in NODE 6 (largest index first).
#
# NODE 6 — IN-PLACE REMOVAL  (basePullAt(array, sorted)) — MUTATES `array`:
#   Walk `sorted` from its LAST element to its FIRST (descending position):
#       length = array ? sorted.length : 0; lastIndex = length - 1
#       for pos = lastIndex down to 0:
#           index = sorted[pos]
#           if (pos == lastIndex) OR (index !== previous):   # skip ADJACENT duplicates
#               previous = index
#               if isIndex(index):                            # valid array index (length defaulted to MAX_SAFE_INTEGER here)
#                   array.splice(index, 1)                    # remove 1 element, shifting tail left
#               else if not isKey(index, array):              # deep path string  (NOT in-scope; see OUT-OF-ENVELOPE)
#                   delete deep property via castPath/parent
#               else:                                         # shallow non-index key
#                   delete array[index]                       # for an OOR/negative number this deletes a NON-existent prop => NO-OP
#   *** Because `sorted` is ascending and we walk back-to-front, we splice the
#       HIGHEST index FIRST, so removing it does NOT shift the positions of the
#       lower indexes still to be removed. This is the load-bearing ordering. ***
#   *** Consecutive-equal sorted entries are DEDUPED (a duplicated index removes
#       only ONCE), but the duplicate STILL contributed its value to `result` in
#       NODE 4. e.g. pullAt(['a','b','c','d'], 1, 1):
#           result = ['b','b']  (value read twice)
#           post   = ['a','c','d']  (removed once)
#       Dedup is ONLY of ADJACENT-equal entries in the SORTED list; a value that
#       appears non-adjacently after sort is removed each non-adjacent occurrence.  ***
#   *** Out-of-range / negative / fractional indexes fail isIndex AND (being
#       numbers) are isKey-true, so they hit the `delete array[index]` branch which
#       deletes a NON-EXISTENT property => NO observable change to `array`. So they
#       contribute `undefined` to `result` and remove NOTHING. ***
#   For an EMPTY `array` ([]): length capture = 0, isIndex(_, 0) is always false
#   (length falsy), so NODE 5 leaves every index uncoerced; in NODE 6 isIndex(index)
#   with default length is still false for non-integer/OOR but TRUE for a plain
#   non-negative-integer number (e.g. 0) — however splice(0,1) on [] is a no-op, so
#   post stays []; every requested index yields undefined in `result`.
#
# NODE 7 — RETURN `result` (the removed-values array from NODE 4). NOT `array`.

# =====================================================================
# WORKED EXAMPLES (verified against the real package — see PROVENANCE)
# =====================================================================
#   pullAt([5,10,15,20], 1, 3)        -> result [10,20]        post [5,15]
#   pullAt([5,10,15,20], [1,3])       -> result [10,20]        post [5,15]        # array-form flattened one level
#   pullAt([0,1,2,3,4], 0, 2, 4)      -> result [0,2,4]        post [1,3]         # descending removal, no shift bug
#   pullAt([0,1,2,3,4], 4, 2, 0)      -> result [4,2,0]        post [1,3]         # result keeps INPUT order; post identical to above
#   pullAt(['w','x','y','z'], 3,0,1)  -> result ['z','w','x']  post ['y']         # result order = input order, NOT sorted
#   pullAt(['a','b','c','d'], 1, 1)   -> result ['b','b']      post ['a','c','d'] # dup: read twice, removed once
#   pullAt([0,1,2,3], 0, 2, 0)        -> result [0,2,0]        post [1,3]         # sort->[0,0,2]; back-to-front: splice 2, splice 0, skip adjacent 0
#   pullAt([10,20,30,40], 2, 2)       -> result [30,30]        post [10,20,40]    # adjacent dup removed once
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], 10)         -> result [undefined]    post ['a','b','c'] # out-of-range: undef, no removal
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], 3)          -> result [undefined]    post ['a','b','c'] # index == length is out-of-range
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], -1)         -> result [undefined]    post ['a','b','c'] # NEGATIVE does NOT count from end; out-of-range
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], 1.5)        -> result [undefined]    post ['a','b','c'] # fractional: not an index
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], '1')        -> result ['b']          post ['a','c']     # numeric-string index works
#   pullAt(['a','b','c','d'], -1, 1)  -> result [undefined,'b'] post ['a','c','d']
#   pullAt([], 0, 1)                  -> result [undefined,undefined] post []
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'])             -> result []             post ['a','b','c'] # no indexes: nothing removed
#   pullAt(['a','b','c'], 0, 1, 2)    -> result ['a','b','c']  post []            # remove everything
#   pullAt([1,'foo',true,null,2.5],0,4,2) -> result [1,2.5,true] post ['foo',null] # mixed value types; result follows input order
#
# NOTE ON SEMANTIC HINT: the upstream task hint said "negative indexes count from
# the end". That is FALSE for _.pullAt (verified): a negative index is out-of-range,
# yields `undefined`, and removes nothing. The SOURCE is authoritative.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json   # observe = result+post (auto-stamped by factory; KIND STATE)

# =====================================================================
# SCOPE
# =====================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the whole NODE 1..7 pipeline above —
#   rest-gather, flatten-one-level, baseAt result (PRE-mutation, original order,
#   undefined for missing/OOR/negative/fractional), isIndex test, ascending sort,
#   back-to-front splice with adjacent-dedup, in-place mutation of the SAME array,
#   return of the new removed-values array.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. (No tables; the only constants are MAX_SAFE_INTEGER =
#   9007199254740991 and the regex /^(?:0|[1-9]\d*)$/ used by isIndex, both inlined
#   above. No carried.json.)
#
# DEMONSTRATED IN-SCOPE INPUT SET (self-consistent with genInputs):
#   - `array`: a real Array of JSON-round-trippable primitives / nested plain
#     arrays / plain objects (numbers, strings, booleans, null). May be empty.
#   - each index arg: a non-negative integer number, a negative integer number,
#     a fractional number, a numeric STRING ("1"), OR a one-level Array of such.
#   Every such value's behavior is fully defined by NODES 1..7 above.
#
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented; NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   - `array` being null/undefined/non-array: baseRest still runs; length=0; result
#     is all-undefined; basePullAt no-ops. Excluded to keep the observable a clean
#     {result, post} over a real array.
#   - Deep STRING path indexes (e.g. 'a.b.c') and non-numeric string keys: drive the
#     isKey/castPath/parent/delete branch in NODE 6. In scope of the algorithm but
#     NOT emitted (pullAt on an Array with a string path is a degenerate lodash use).
#   - Array-VALUED index elements that SURVIVE flatten as arrays (e.g. a doubly
#     nested [[ [1], 2 ]]): isIndex coerces them via String() (e.g. [1] -> "1"),
#     producing exotic but defined behavior. Documented, NOT emitted.
#   - Element VALUES that are functions / Symbols / undefined: not JSON-codec-faithful
#     for `result`/`post` capture. NOT emitted. (null, booleans, numbers, strings,
#     nested plain arrays/objects of primitives ARE fine.)

PROVENANCE lodash.pullat@4.6.0  (repo lodash/lodash, tag 4.6.0-npm-packages/lodash.pullat, MIT)
